/**
@defgroup tools check, split and print CBOR bytes

The functions of this group check, split and print CBOR bytes. Each
function reads the bytes that the caller passes. Include `<cbor/cbor.hpp>`.

`cbor::item_size` gives the size of the first item in the bytes. `cbor::sequence`
gives the items of a CBOR sequence (RFC 8742) one by one.
`cbor::sequence_reader` does the same for a sequence whose bytes arrive in
pieces. `cbor::diagnostic_notation` prints an item as text.

@section tools_size ITEM SIZE

`cbor::item_size(encoded)` gives the size in bytes of the first item in
`encoded`, as a `std::expected<std::size_t, cbor::error>`. It checks that
the item is well-formed. It copies nothing and allocates nothing. Bytes
after the item do not change the result.

The call does not recurse. The nesting depth of the item does not limit
it. An item whose head uses indefinite length gives `indefinite_length`.

@section tools_sequence A CBOR SEQUENCE

`cbor::sequence` reads a CBOR sequence (RFC 8742). A CBOR sequence holds
top-level items one after the other, with nothing between them. Each
element of the range is a `std::expected<std::string_view, cbor::error>`:
the bytes of one item, as a view into the bytes of the owner. The sequence
keeps the owner alive.

The owner is a `std::shared_ptr<std::string const>`. It is also a
`std::shared_ptr<void const>` together with a view of the bytes that it
keeps alive. A null pointer throws `std::logic_error`.

An item that is not well-formed gives its error. The range ends after that
element. A last item that is not complete gives `too_little_data`. The
iterator gives the bytes from the current item to the end of the sequence
through `encoded()`.

The sequence is an input range. It has no size. `begin()` reads the first
item again.

Each item is a top-level item of its own. A tag 29 in one item does not
reach a tag 28 in another item. `cbor::lazy::view(owner, item)` reads one
item in place, where `owner` is the shared pointer of the sequence.

@section tools_reader READ A SEQUENCE IN PIECES

`cbor::sequence_reader` reads a CBOR sequence whose bytes arrive in
pieces, for example from a socket. A piece can end in the middle of an
item.

- `feed(piece)` gives the reader the next bytes.
- `next()` gives the next item as a `std::expected<cbor::lazy, cbor::error>`,
  when all of its bytes are there. While it waits for more bytes, it gives
  `too_little_data`. The reader continues where it stopped.
- `empty()` gives `true` when every byte that was fed is in an item that
  `next` gave.

`feed` takes the pieces in these forms:

- a `std::string` that you move: the reader takes it without a copy;
- a `std::shared_ptr` to a container of bytes: the reader keeps it without
  a copy. A null pointer throws `std::logic_error`;
- a `std::string_view`, or a `std::string` that you do not move: the reader
  copies the bytes.

A literal does not compile, because it ends with a NUL byte. A
`std::shared_ptr` to bytes that are not const does not compile.

Each item is a `cbor::lazy` that keeps its bytes alive. The item stays valid
after later calls of `feed` and after the reader ends. An item that is
whole in a piece that the reader holds is a view into that piece. The reader
copies only the bytes of an item that the end of a piece cuts. It holds the
bytes of an item until all of them arrive.

The reader does not recurse. No nesting depth limits it.

After an error other than `too_little_data`, every call of `next` gives that
error, and `feed` keeps no byte. The reader cannot be copied. A call on a
reader that was moved from throws `std::logic_error`.

@section tools_print PRINT AS DIAGNOSTIC NOTATION

`cbor::diagnostic_notation(encoded)` gives the item in `encoded` as text, in
the diagnostic notation of RFC 8949 section 8. The text looks like JSON. The
result is a `std::expected<std::string, cbor::error>`. The bytes `encoded`
hold exactly one item. A byte after the item gives `syntax_error`.

- A head that is not in preferred form carries its encoding indicator. The
  bytes `19 00 01` give `1_1`.
- An item of indefinite length is printed with `_`. The bytes `9f 01 ff`
  give `[_ 1]`.
- A tag is printed as its number and its content. The bytes `c1 00` give
  `1(0)`. Tag 29 is not resolved.
- The call does not check tag content, UTF-8 or duplicate keys.

The bytes `a1 61 61 83 01 f9 41 00 41 01` give the text
`{"a": [1, 2.5, h'01']}`.

@section tools_errors ERRORS

Each function gives a `std::expected` with a `cbor::error`. A wrong use
throws `std::logic_error`: a null pointer for `cbor::sequence` or for `feed`,
and a call on a `cbor::sequence_reader` that was moved from.

- `too_little_data`: the bytes end before the item ends, or a count is
  larger than the bytes that are left.
- `syntax_error`: a head is not well-formed, or bytes follow the item in
  `diagnostic_notation`.
- `indefinite_length`: a head uses indefinite length, in `item_size` and in
  `sequence_reader`.
- `string_length_exceeded`: a string is longer than `limits::string_length`,
  in `item_size` and in `diagnostic_notation`.
- `container_elements_exceeded`: the count of an array or a map is greater
  than `limits::container_elements`, in `item_size` and in
  `diagnostic_notation`.
- `nesting_depth_exceeded`: `diagnostic_notation` of an item that is deeper
  than the stack of the thread holds.

@manonly
.SH "EXAMPLES"
@endmanonly
@snippet doc_reference_tools.cpp tools group
*/
